1	// The client-side cache. Browser-only, and the reason a fleet-wide dashboard is
2	// affordable at all: without it, one page load across a dozen members spends
3	// several hundred API calls of a 5,000/hour budget, and a second look a minute
4	// later spends them again.
5	//
6	// THREE STRATEGIES, because the data has three different shapes and one TTL over
7	// all of them would be wrong in both directions:
8	//
9	//   IMMUTABLE  — repo content at a commit sha. A path at a sha cannot change, so
10	//                this is cached under the sha and never revalidated. The task
11	//                declarations are the whole win here: unchanged `main` means zero
12	//                calls to re-read every task.json.
13	//   VALIDATED  — anything live (open items, runs). Cached WITH its ETag and
14	//                re-requested with `If-None-Match`. GitHub answers 304 when
15	//                nothing changed, and a 304 does not count against the rate limit,
16	//                so this is free freshness rather than stale data.
17	//   AGEING     — closed history, which is settled but not addressable by a sha.
18	//                Plain TTL, 24h by default.
19	//
20	// Storage is `localStorage`, holding COMPACT PROJECTIONS rather than API payloads:
